#!/usr/bin/env bash
# pcmanfm-qt — shim forcing XWayland, shadowing the real binary.
#
# THE BUG THIS WORKS AROUND
# --------------------------
# NEXT-SESSION.md, measured with scripts/test/dnd-peer.py and uinput-shake.py,
# driven between two windows of the SAME test program — not an app bug:
#
#     A WAYLAND DRAG DOES NOT REACH AN XWAYLAND WINDOW INTACT. The drop
#     arrives and carries the X11 PRIMARY selection instead of the URI that
#     was offered.
#
# qdrop.py positions itself on open, which only XWayland permits a client to
# do (GDK_BACKEND=x11, see qdrop.sh) — so it is always on the XWayland side of
# that broken bridge. pcmanfm-qt runs QT_QPA_PLATFORM=wayland;xcb, i.e. it
# prefers native Wayland and gets it — putting the file manager you would
# actually drag from on the WRONG side. That mismatch, not anything wrong
# with qdrop's own drag/drop code, is why dragging a file INTO qdrop.py from
# pcmanfm-qt never worked, and it is documented at length as the reason a
# previous session rebuilt the shelf in Quickshell instead of fixing this.
#
# THE OTHER FIX, NOT TRIED THERE
# --------------------------------
# The bridge is broken in ONE direction: Wayland source into XWayland
# destination. It is NOT broken XWayland-to-XWayland — the same measurements
# record "XWayland source -> GTK shelf: drop in and drag out both work".
# Moving pcmanfm-qt onto XWayland too, instead of moving qdrop off it, takes
# both ends of the drag off the broken bridge entirely rather than routing
# around one side of it.
#
# The usual cost of forcing a Qt app onto XWayland is that it stops seeing
# fractional scaling (see hyprland.conf's own note on this). Irrelevant here:
# `hyprctl monitors` reports scale=1 on this machine's one output, so there
# is no fractional scale to lose.
#
# WHY A SHIM AND NOT A .desktop OVERRIDE
# -----------------------------------------
# pcmanfm-qt starts from more than one place — the $mod M scratchpad bind,
# theme-apply's daemon-mode restart, a launcher entry — and a .desktop
# override only catches the launcher path. Shadowing the binary itself via
# /usr/local/bin (AtiScriptsV1/install.sh puts every file in this directory
# there, ahead of /usr/bin in $PATH — the same mechanism the `rofi` shim
# uses) catches all of them uniformly, with nothing to keep in sync.
set -euo pipefail

export QT_QPA_PLATFORM=xcb
exec /usr/bin/pcmanfm-qt "$@"
